    "collapsible_content_home_faq": {
      "type": "collapsible-content",
      "blocks": {
        "collapsible_tab_hW7crK": {
          "type": "collapsible_tab",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How long before I notice anything?",
            "open": false,
            "content": "<p>The afternoon energy crashes usually shifts first, often within the first two weeks. Steadier energy and quieter cravings tend to follow.</p><p>That's because when your insulin signal is low, glucose stays in your blood instead of reaching your cells — so you're tired despite having eaten. As the signalling improves, that fuel actually gets used.</p><p>Cycles take longer because insulin has to come down before your androgens fall, then follicles develop. Most studies runs 12 weeks because that's how long ovulation and testosterone realistically need. Stop at four weeks and you haven't given it a fair go.</p><p></p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_tab_a3zDiF": {
          "type": "collapsible_tab",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why isn't there vitamin D or folate in this?",
            "open": false,
            "content": "<p>Because you're probably already taking them.</p><p>Vitamin D should be dosed to your own blood levels, with it's cofactors K2 and magnesium alongside it — not a fixed amount somebody guessed at. Folate is already in your B-complex or prenatal, usually at a deliberate dose.</p><p>Add a second source of either and you've lost track of your total dose. We'd rather do one thing at the right dose and leave the rest of your routine to you.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_tab_H9cWGH": {
          "type": "collapsible_tab",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Does this product contain corn?",
            "open": false,
            "content": "<p><strong>Our myo-inositol is derived from non-GMO corn.<br/><br/></strong>The myo-inositol is extracted from the corn, and the corn itself is left behind. If you have a corn sensitivity, we’d still recommend being mindful and checking with your doctor before you start.<br/><br/>Much of the corn grown today is genetically modified, so we only source from non-GMO corn. Corn is currently the most established source of myo-inositol, and we’ve chosen the highest-quality supply we could find.<br/><br/><strong>Our D-chiro-inositol is different. It comes from carob pods, where D-chiro occurs in higher amounts than anywhere else in nature.</strong> D-chiro does a lot of the heavy lifting on estrogen and androgen balance, so where it comes from matters.<br/><br/>Most brands make their D-chiro by converting myo-inositol from corn in a lab. We extract ours from carob instead. That keeps it as close to the source as possible. It’s also why our powder is naturally sweet and soft in texture, without any added flavor.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_tab_KdyDNX": {
          "type": "collapsible_tab",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What is GLUT4?",
            "open": false,
            "content": "<p>GLUT4 is a protein that helps transport glucose into cells where it can be used for energy. Inositol supports optimal GLUT4 activation, which can improve glucose metabolism.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_tab_aTQyqe": {
          "type": "collapsible_tab",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What makes Bodology different from other inositol supplements?",
            "open": false,
            "content": "<p>We’re a female-led, <a href=\"/pages/meet-our-experts\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Meet Our Experts\">practitioner-backed</a> brand that formulates clean, high-quality inositol based on science and real-world experience. Unlike mass brands or male-led supplement companies, we design with hormonal health, fertility, and women’s lived experiences in mind. While others provide <a href=\"/blogs/bodytalks/which-is-better-inositol-powder-or-pills\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Which is Better: Inositol Powder Or Pills?\">capsules</a> that underdose or add ingredients like DIM or folate (which may not suit PCOS or TTC), our blend is clean, bioavailable, and practitioner-trusted.</p><p></p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_tab_ceQNA8": {
          "type": "collapsible_tab",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How is this different from berberine?",
            "open": false,
            "content": "<p>They work in different places.<br/><br/>Berberine acts through AMPK — an energy-sensing pathway. Inositol works on insulin signalling itself: the message that tells your cells to take glucose in.<br/><br/>If berberine gave you something and then plateaued, that's usually why. It isn't a weaker version of this. It's aimed at a different part of the system. Plenty of women take both.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_tab_gMPJnb": {
          "type": "collapsible_tab",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why powder and not capsules?",
            "open": false,
            "content": "<p>The studied dose is 4 grams a day. In capsule form, that's eight to ten capsules.</p><p>Powder is the only format where the full dose is realistic to take.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_tab_997YEj": {
          "type": "collapsible_tab",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What does it taste like? Does it actually dissolve?",
            "open": false,
            "content": "<p>Gently sweet on its own — that's the inositol and carob sourced d-chiro, not added sweetener. There's nothing else in the tub to taste.<br/><br/>It dissolves clear, with no grit and no aftertaste, because there are no fillers or flow agents to leave residue. Water, coffee, a smoothie, whatever you already drink. <br/><br/>Some women skip the liquid and take it straight off the scoop.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_tab_zGJYxY": {
          "type": "collapsible_tab",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Can I take it with metformin, my prenatal, or a GLP-1?",
            "open": false,
            "content": "<p>Generally yes. Inositol isn't a hormone and it doesn't block anything — it's a compound your body already produces and uses, taken at a higher amount.</p><p>Metformin works mainly on glucose output from the liver, so it addresses a different part of the picture. That's why the two are often used alongside each other rather than as alternatives. The same applies to prenatals and GLP-1s.</p><p>If you're taking prescription medication, run it past your doctor first.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_tab_LctEEj": {
          "type": "collapsible_tab",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Do I need a PCOS diagnosis for this to make sense?",
            "open": false,
            "content": "<p>No. Inositol works on insulin signalling, and insulin resistance often develops years before anyone puts a name to it.</p><p>If you're tired after meals, reaching for something sweet mid-afternoon, or finding that real effort returns less than it should — the mechanism applies whether or not it's written on a chart.</p><p>Worth knowing which tests catch it: fasting insulin and HOMA-IR. Fasting glucose tends to stay normal until much later, because your pancreas compensates by producing more insulin. That compensation is the problem — and it's the reason so many women are told everything looks fine.</p><h3></h3>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_tab_rD7wfQ": {
          "type": "collapsible_tab",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Are there any side effects?",
            "open": false,
            "content": "<p>Inositol has a strong safety record. A small number of women notice some bloating or a change in digestion in the first few days, usually because 4g is a meaningful amount to introduce at once.<br/><br/>If that's you, start with one scoop and build to two over a week. It typically settles.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_tab_CBzneK": {
          "type": "collapsible_tab",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What if it doesn't work for me?",
            "open": false,
            "content": "<p>You have 60 days. If it isn't doing anything for you, email us and we'll refund you — no phone call, no back-and-forth, no attempt to talk you out of it. Bought a bundle? Your first tub is covered by our 60-day guarantee, and any unopened tubs after that can be returned to us for a refund.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        }
      },
